name 'pl-aif-y2-u1-l3-defining-functions'
title 'title'
description 'description here'

markdown <<MARKDOWN
<h1>Defining Functions</h1>

<p>Repeating the same steps over and over gets tedious to write out every time. Here's the same two workouts logged two different ways, same outcome either way.</p>

<div style="margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; color: #1F1976; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.04em; margin-bottom: 8px;">Without a Function</div>
  <div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
    <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 18px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8;">
      <div style="line-height: 2;">print("Workout complete!")</div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">print("You walked 2000 steps.")</div>
      <div style="height: 16px;"></div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">print("Workout complete!")</div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">print("You walked 3000 steps.")</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; padding: 12px 18px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #1F1976;">
      <div style="line-height: 1.9;">Workout complete!</div>
      <div style="line-height: 1.9;">You walked 2000 steps.</div>
      <div style="line-height: 1.9;">Workout complete!</div>
      <div style="line-height: 1.9;">You walked 3000 steps.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; color: #1F1976; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.04em; margin-bottom: 8px;">With a Function</div>
  <div style="border-radius: 10px; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8;">
    <div style="background-color: #1F1976; padding: 16px 18px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8;">
      <div style="line-height: 2;">def log_workout(steps):</div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">&nbsp;&nbsp;print("Workout complete!")</div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">&nbsp;&nbsp;print(f"You walked {steps} steps.")</div>
      <div style="height: 16px;"></div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">log_workout(2000)</div>
      <div style="line-height: 2;">log_workout(3000)</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; padding: 12px 18px; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #1F1976;">
      <div style="line-height: 1.9;">Workout complete!</div>
      <div style="line-height: 1.9;">You walked 2000 steps.</div>
      <div style="line-height: 1.9;">Workout complete!</div>
      <div style="line-height: 1.9;">You walked 3000 steps.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="line-height: 1.1;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">Identical output, but the function version writes the repeated logic once and reuses it. <code style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1; padding: 0 4px;">def</code>, short for "define," starts a function. Everything belonging to it is indented beneath it, that's how Python knows what's inside and what isn't. Once defined, calling it by name, <code style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1; padding: 0 4px;">log_workout(2000)</code>, runs that code.</div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128161; Teacher tip:</strong> if students haven't indented code before, this is the moment. In Python, indentation isn't a style choice, it's required syntax. Expect inconsistent indentation to be a real source of errors here.
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Parameters: Giving a Function Its Input</h2>

<p>Right now, <code style="font-size:16px;">log_workout</code> can log any number of steps without being rewritten. The piece that makes that possible is the parameter.</p>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128214; Parameter:</strong> a variable that holds a function's input.
  </div>
</div>

<p>Here's the same function again, with the parameter marked in both places it appears.</p>

<div style="background-color: #1F1976; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 16px 0; font-family: 'Consolas', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 16px; color: #E4E2F8;">
  <div style="line-height: 2;">def log_workout(<span style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; border-bottom: 3px solid #34BD43; padding: 1px 4px; border-radius: 3px;">steps</span>):</div>
  <div style="line-height: 2;">&nbsp;&nbsp;print("Workout complete!")</div>
  <div style="line-height: 2;">&nbsp;&nbsp;print(f"You walked {<span style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; border-bottom: 3px solid #34BD43; padding: 1px 4px; border-radius: 3px;">steps</span>} steps.")</div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>How it works:</strong>
  </div>
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 8px;">1. <strong>The parameter is a placeholder.</strong> <code style="font-size:16px;">steps</code> in the <code style="font-size:16px;">def</code> line is a variable the function can use inside its body.</div>
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 6px;">2. <strong>The call fills it in.</strong> <code style="font-size:16px;">log_workout(2000)</code> sets <code style="font-size:16px;">steps</code> to 2000 for that run. <code style="font-size:16px;">log_workout(3000)</code> sets it to 3000.</div>
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin-top: 6px;">3. <strong>Same function, different input.</strong> That's why each call prints a different number.</div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128161; Teacher tip:</strong> some students think a parameter has to match a variable they already made. It doesn't. It's just a name the function uses for whatever value gets passed in, so <code style="font-size:16px;">steps</code> could be called anything.
  </div>
</div>
MARKDOWN
